{{!< default}}
{{!-- The Land — origin story. Kanchenjunga Deep hero, SVG district map, ropani lattice diagram.
     Mobile: hero 70svh, map block stacks map-first, gallery 1-col. --}}

<main id="main" class="land-page">
    {{#post}}

    <section class="land-hero">
        {{#if feature_image}}
            <div class="land-hero-bg" style="background-image: url({{img_url feature_image size="xl"}});" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        {{/if}}
        {{> "site-nav" navClass="on-dark"}}
        <div class="land-hero-inner">
            <p class="eyebrow on-dark-eyebrow">The Land · Taplejung</p>
            <h1>{{title}}</h1>
            <p class="coords">27°21′N &nbsp;·&nbsp; 87°40′E &nbsp;·&nbsp; 800–2,000 m</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <div class="land-body">

        <section class="land-map-block">
            <figure class="land-map fade-up" role="img" aria-label="Schematic map of eastern Nepal showing the cardamom district Taplejung, and the trade route to Birgunj">
                <svg viewBox="0 0 520 400" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true">
                    <!-- Eastern Nepal, simplified. Palette only. -->
                    <rect width="520" height="400" fill="#EFE5D0"/>
                    <path d="M20 260 L60 200 L110 215 L150 160 L210 175 L260 120 L320 140 L370 90 L430 110 L480 70 L500 130 L490 210 L500 290 L440 330 L370 315 L300 345 L230 330 L160 355 L90 335 L30 320 Z"
                          fill="#E8DBBF" stroke="#1A1613" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                    <!-- Kanchenjunga ridge -->
                    <path d="M400 95 L425 60 L450 100 M430 100 L455 68 L478 105" fill="none" stroke="#3B4A3F" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"/>
                    <text x="405" y="45" font-family="Manrope, sans-serif" font-size="11" letter-spacing="2" fill="#3B4A3F">KANCHENJUNGA</text>
                    <!-- Growing districts (copper) -->
                    <circle cx="415" cy="150" r="7" fill="#B8703A"/>
                    <text x="430" y="154" font-family="Manrope, sans-serif" font-size="12" letter-spacing="2" fill="#1A1613">TAPLEJUNG</text>
                    <!-- Border exit (ink) -->
                    <circle cx="150" cy="330" r="6" fill="#1A1613"/>
                    <text x="120" y="352" font-family="Manrope, sans-serif" font-size="12" letter-spacing="2" fill="#1A1613">BIRGUNJ</text>
                    <!-- The route that erases a name -->
                    <path d="M415 150 C 340 230, 260 290, 156 326" fill="none" stroke="#8B2E2A" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="6 5"/>
                    <text x="200" y="255" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="13" font-style="italic" fill="#8B2E2A" transform="rotate(-14 200 255)">the road that erases a name</text>
                </svg>
                <figcaption class="legend"><span>● Cardamom districts</span><span>● Commodity exit</span><span>― ― The unnamed road</span></figcaption>
            </figure>

            <div class="land-explain fade-up">
                <h2>It refuses <em>flat farms.</em></h2>
                <p>Cardamom is a shade-loving forest crop. It will not grow in tidy grids under open sun. It wants the wet folds of the Kanchenjunga foothills — under the alder trees, beside the streams, between 800 and 2,000 metres.</p>
                <p>That is why it grows here and almost nowhere else in Nepal at scale: Taplejung. Hills too steep for tractors, tended entirely by hand, one ropani at a time.</p>
                <p>And that is why the last number hurts: nearly everything grown here leaves through Birgunj as an unnamed commodity — graded, sacked, and renamed at the border.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section class="ropani-explainer fade-up">
            <div class="ropani-diagram" role="img" aria-label="Diagram of one ropani: a square of sixteen aana, about 74 feet per side">
                <span class="label">One Ropani</span>
                <div class="grid" aria-hidden="true">
                    <b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b><b></b>
                </div>
                <span class="dim w">≈ 74 ft</span>
                <span class="dim h">≈ 74 ft</span>
            </div>
            <div class="text">
                <h3>The <em>ropani</em> — how a hill measures itself.</h3>
                <p>Nepal's hills do not count in hectares. A ropani is about 508.7 square metres — sixteen aana, roughly a square of seventy-four feet. When a farmer tells you "fifteen ropani," they are telling you the size of their world.</p>
                <p>Every farmer on this site is counted in ropani, because that is how they count themselves.</p>
                <div class="facts">
                    <div class="f"><div class="n">508.7</div><div class="l">square metres</div></div>
                    <div class="f"><div class="n">16</div><div class="l">aana</div></div>
                    <div class="f"><div class="n">19.7</div><div class="l">ropani ≈ 1 hectare</div></div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        {{#if content}}
        <section class="art-body land-content">
            {{content}}
        </section>
        {{/if}}

        {{#if @custom.land_gallery_1}}
        <section class="land-gallery fade-up">
            <div class="cell"><img src="{{img_url @custom.land_gallery_1 size="l"}}" alt="Cardamom growing under the alder trees" loading="lazy"></div>
            <div class="side">
                {{#if @custom.land_gallery_2}}<div class="cell"><img src="{{img_url @custom.land_gallery_2 size="m"}}" alt="The cardamom plant up close" loading="lazy"></div>{{/if}}
                {{#if @custom.land_gallery_3}}<div class="cell"><img src="{{img_url @custom.land_gallery_3 size="m"}}" alt="Hillside terraces in eastern Nepal" loading="lazy"></div>{{/if}}
            </div>
        </section>
        {{/if}}

        <section class="photo-direction fade-up">
            <div><h4>The light</h4><p>Warm-shadow, 3200K, low-key. Bhatti scenes are lit by fire only. Morning mist is allowed; drone-brochure panoramas are not.</p></div>
            <div><h4>The land</h4><p>Steep, wet, worked. Terraces with tools left in them. No empty postcard hills — this land is someone's fifteen ropani.</p></div>
            <div><h4>The people</h4><p>Portraits 3:4, quarter-turn, hands visible, natural light. Never a forced smile. The person is named or the photograph is not used.</p></div>
        </section>

        <section class="page-close">
            {{> "subscribe-card"}}
        </section>

    </div>
    {{/post}}
</main>
